  "executive_briefing": "The corporate and international tax landscape in late 2026 is experiencing structural adjustments driven by systemic implementation of multilateral agreements, digital tax administrative enforcement, and volatile tariff regimes. Over 140 countries are committed to or actively implementing the OECD Pillar Two 15% global minimum tax rules (GloBE), altering tax planning for multinational enterprises. While statutory rates shift incrementally, average effective tax rates (EATR) stand globally at 20.5%, balancing national competitiveness with new base erosion baselines, exemplified by Singapore's SGD 2,000 cash grants and 50% CIT rebates for the 2026 assessment year. Concurrently, digital tax compliance transitions from policy to practice as seen with Singapore's mandatory e-invoicing InvoiceNow network rollout. Meanwhile, trade policy remains highly volatile, with major economies executing reciprocal tariffs and trade restrictions, forcing multinationals to adopt complex compliance tracking tools.",
